  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1037 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe growing season has come to an end this morning as high pressure \nwill remain in control over the FA through Monday morning. Lows\nwill not be as chilly, ranging from the mid 30s to near 40 \ndegrees. Due to a modest increase in dewpoints today with \nsoutherly return flow, more widespread patchy fog will be \npossible late tonight into Monday morning, with the greatest \nrisk near bodies of water. Otherwise, pleasant weather \nconditions with ample sunshine will start off the week as \ntemperatures trend 5 to 10 degrees above normal through \nWednesday. Highs will range from the upper 60s to lower 70s with\nlows only in the 40s. \n\nAs a 500 mb vort max ejects downstream from Iowa on Wednesday, \nmodel guidance is now in better agreement with a 1004 mb sfc \nlow pressure system developing over central Illinois by \nWednesday evening. The low will be associated with a strong \ncold front that is progged to sweep across the FA. Timing \nremains pretty consistent for rain showers, with a 40-60% chance\ndeveloping Wednesday afternoon through Wednesday evening. \nRainfall amounts on ensemble guidance generally range between a \ntenth to a quarter of an inch on average. \n\nBehind the front, a highly amplified trough settles over the Ohio \nValley bringing the coldest airmass of the season through the end of \nthe week. Residual 700 mb moisture on Thursday with prevailing \nnorthwest flow will support lingering sprinkles due to strong \nlift aloft. Wind gusts between 20-30 mph will also be possible \nwith a rapid increase in cyclogenesis to the east. If moisture \ncan linger into Thursday night, it would not be out of the \nquestion a wet snow flurry mixes in given extremely cold air \naloft. While highs on Thursday are progged in the 50s, there is \nnow a 60-80% chance of highs only in the 40s on Friday before \ntemperatures begin to rebound over next weekend. Lows by the NBM\nnext Saturday morning are currently progged in the upper 20s.",
